  "worked_solution": "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=3$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x-3}{\\sqrt{3x-1}-\\sqrt{8}} & =\\frac{3-3}{\\sqrt{3(3)-1}-\\sqrt{8}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-3}{\\sqrt{8}-\\sqrt{8}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x-3}{\\sqrt{3x-1}-\\sqrt{8}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(x-3)(\\sqrt{3x-1}+\\sqrt{8})}{(\\sqrt{3x-1}-\\sqrt{8})(\\sqrt{3x-1}+\\sqrt{8})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(x-3)(\\sqrt{3x-1}+\\sqrt{8})}{(\\sqrt{3x-1})^{2}-(\\sqrt{8})^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(x-3)(\\sqrt{3x-1}+\\sqrt{8})}{3x-1-8} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(x-3)(\\sqrt{3x-1}+\\sqrt{8})}{3(x-3)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\sqrt{3x-1}+\\sqrt{8}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3(3)-1}+\\sqrt{8}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{8}+\\sqrt{8}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{8}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
